5.1 VLAN questions.

These are questions and answers about VLANs:

1. Why is a "flat" switch (a switch without VLANs) a bad idea for security, AND why is it slower?

Ans: The reason "flat" switches without VLANs are bad for security is if a hacker plugs in a compromised computer, or if you even plug in a vulnerable IOT device, then that 
device now has access to all the IP Addresses, can be a rogue DHCP server, listen in on traffick, flooding the network with fake traffick to DOS it, or potentially even 
infecting other computers with malware

It's also slower because broadcast addresses are sent to EVERY single computer on the network, there is no segmentation.

2. What even is a VLAN?

Ans: A VLAN is a virtual Local Area Network, it means making the switch pretend there are two different networks, two different routers. It basically splits a network in half 
(or four or more...)

3. What is the default VLAN and what are the most common names of ones businesses typically make?

Ans: The default VLAN is VLAN 1, and all data travels through this unless you change the VLAN. Other names are Data (customer traffick), Management (administrators who own 
the VLANs), Voice (Voice over IP), Native VLAN (Changing the default VLAN to Native, mostly to screw up and confuse hackers, but all security helps right?), and Black Hole 
(A useless VLAN, that is set to unused ports for security so nobody can plug in a new PC without approval.)
